\title{Per-Example Predictive Multiplicity in Hybrid Linear Recurrent Architectures}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Adding attention to linear recurrent architectures is associated with per-example changes on almost entirely different examples across three controlled hybrid linear recurrent designs: pairwise overlap is at most 10\%. Using the model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid} (three architectures, five attention ratios, two scales), we decompose per-example effects and find that this predictive multiplicity \citep{marx2020predictive} depends on task type ($\alpha = 0.14$--$0.30$) rather than attention dose. Aggregate trends do not reach statistical significance ($n = 5$; Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .170$), yet per-example overlap saturates at 4\% attention and remains flat across all four benchmarks. Naive majority vote fails to exploit this complementarity, suggesting that configurations interchangeable by aggregate metrics carry hidden consequences for per-example reliability.
\end{abstract}

\input{sections/introduction}

\input{sections/related_work}

\input{sections/method}

\input{sections/experiments}

\input{sections/conclusion}

\section*{Limitations}

Our analysis has several limitations.

\paragraph{Single training runs.} Each configuration corresponds to a single training run; we cannot fully disentangle architectural from seed-specific effects, though the $3\times$ within- vs.\ cross-architecture Jaccard gap (\S\ref{ssec:decoupling}) provides proxy evidence. All per-example claims are conditioned on this limitation. With $n = 5$ attention ratios, the minimum achievable $p$-value is 0.017, contributing to LAMBADA's Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .170$ not reaching conventional significance. As a partial calibration, we compare within-seed Jaccard from Pythia 70M PolyPythias \citep{vanderwal2025polypythias} against our cross-architecture values (Appendix~\ref{app:seed_calibration}); within-seed overlap is 2.7--4.3$\times$ higher, consistent with, but not definitive evidence for, architecture-driven divergence. Future work should conduct multi-seed training on matched architectures to fully isolate architectural from seed-specific effects.

\paragraph{Coverage and generalization.} We examine three primary architectures from one model zoo on four main benchmarks (two supplementary); a fourth architecture (GLA) confirms the pattern at 340M (Appendix~\ref{app:four_arch}). The controlled design isolates architectural effects but limits generalization to other hybrid families. We compare only two scale points (340M, 1.3B). Our analysis does not cover Mamba, RWKV, or generation tasks; generalization to these architectures and task types remains open.

\paragraph{Pre-training only.} All models are evaluated without fine-tuning; per-example effects may not persist after task-specific adaptation. We evaluate only discrete accuracy; continuous metrics may reveal different multiplicity patterns.

\section*{Ethics Statement}

This work analyzes publicly available pretrained language models from the model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}. No new data collection, human subjects research, or model training is involved. We identify per-example behavioral differences across architectures, which may inform practitioners about the limitations of aggregate benchmark comparisons but does not introduce new risks.

\section*{Code and Data Availability}

All models are from the publicly available model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}. Our analysis code and per-example evaluation outputs will be released upon publication.

